1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,240
 Not all ceramics are made by consolidating a powder under pressure before firing.

2
00:00:04,240 --> 00:00:08,560
 In fact, many ceramics are made through a casting process known as slip casting.

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:14,800
 In slip casting, a ceramic powder is mixed with a solvent usually water into a slurry that we call slip.

4
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:18,720
 This slip is poured into a mould at which point surface tension and adhesion between the

5
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:24,480
 slip and the walls of the mould allows a very thin layer of it to kind of set on the outside.

6
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:29,120
 The remaining excess slip is poured out of the mould and this leaves only a thin green body

7
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:33,440
 on the inside. After a drying process, usually inside the mould at a lower temperature,

8
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:38,240
 the green body is removed and allowed to fire or center the same as you would any other technical

9
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:42,480
 non-technical ceramic. Because of the way that it works, the casting is primarily and very commonly

10
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:47,520
 used to make hollow geometries and is used extensively for both technical and non-technical ceramics.

11
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:51,840
 At the industrial scale, slip casting is used to make ceramic tubes and pipes and other

12
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:56,800
 standard sections. Much the same as we had in week 4 when we were talking about supply of metals.

13
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:00,640
 Though of course, as you can imagine, owing to the higher material costs and the higher

14
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:05,600
 complexity involved in the need to fire and center these things, the prices of slip cast ceramic

15
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,400
 tubes are generally far higher than those of metal ones. Compared to pressing slip cast

16
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:14,320
 components are generally a little bit more porous owing to the fact that they haven't had any

17
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:18,960
 pressure applied to help work out some of those pores. And for this reason, they can be weaker

18
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:23,280
 and a little bit more brittle in service. For higher quality slip cast components, there's a lot

19
00:01:23,280 --> 00:01:27,440
 of care that goes into trying to work out some of these pores through higher temperature and

20
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,680
 longer sintering. But this level of care and treatment that's required means that these higher

21
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:36,400
 quality slip cast components are much more expensive on average than pressed ones.

22
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,880
 Slip casting is not only an industrial technical engineering process, it's also used on the

23
00:01:40,880 --> 00:01:44,960
 smaller scale by hobbyists and artists. In this case hobbyists are generally working with

24
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:49,440
 fired non-technical ceramics like borsalins and clays. But some incredibly complicated geometries

25
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:53,120
 can be made this way. To finish up, I'll just note that there's a little bit of a hybrid

26
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:58,160
 between a pressing process and a slip casting process in the production of many fired non-technical

27
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,840
 ceramics like bricks. Here, a thick slurry, a thick slip is made and is extruded through a die.

28
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:07,680
 In this case, you need to make considerations both towards pressing and towards slip casting in

29
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:12,400
 the design of a component. But this is very much more a non-technical civil kind of application,

30
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:16,000
 so I might just leave it there. As always, I'd love some more information and resources

31
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:20,000
 about slip casting on the textbook page that accompanies this video. I encourage you to check them out.
